\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSUnarchiver, a concrete subclass of NSCoder, defines methods for decoding a set of Objective-C objects from an archive. Such archives are produced by objects of the NSArchiver class. See the NSCoder and NSArchiver specifications for an introduction to archiving.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql General Exception Conditions\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 While unarchiving, NSUnarchiver performs a variety of consistency checks on the incoming data stream. NSUnarchiver raises an NSInconsistentArchiveException when: \
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab A class name is missing where one is expected.\
\'b7\tab A class name is found that refers to an unknown class.\
\'b7\tab \i \i0 A type code is found that\'27s different from the one expected.\
\'b7\tab An unknown type code is found.\
\'b7\tab Excess characters are found in a type code, or characters are missing.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 For a description of type codes, see the discussion of the \b @encode()\b0  compiler directive in \i Object-Oriented Programming and the Objective-C Language\i0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 Invoking inappropriate methods can also lead to errors. NSUnarchiver\'27s superclass, NSCoder, provides methods for both encoding and decoding. However, only the decoding methods are applicable to NSUnarchiver; don\'27t send an NSUnarchiver any \b encode...\b0  messages. \
\fs14\
